<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madhavrao I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1745">1745</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Madhavrao Peshwa, Madhav Rao I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madhavrao I (1745–1772) was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> from 1761 to 1772. Coming to office soon after the disaster of Panipat, he restored Maratha power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and the north, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> and led repeated campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, forcing him to cede territory and pay tribute. His early death from tuberculosis at the age of twenty-seven weakened the Peshwa's government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madhavrao-i.jpg" alt="Madhavrao I" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bhoj Raj · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madhavrao_I_Peshwa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Madhavrao was born at Savnur on 15 February 1745, the second son of Peshwa Balaji Bajirao, known as Nanasaheb, and Gopikabai, and a grandson of Bajirao I. In 1753 he married Ramabai, daughter of Trimbakrao Bapusaheb Patwardhan. His elder brother Vishwasrao and his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashivrao-bhau">Sadashivrao Bhau</a> were killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a>, and his father died shortly afterwards. He was declared Peshwa on 21 June 1761, at sixteen, with his paternal uncle Raghunathrao at first acting as regent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1762 Raghunathrao raised an army against him, broke a peace treaty and defeated him at the Battle of Alegaon on 12 November 1762, taking control of the administration with Sakharam Bapu Bokil. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, Raghunathrao's ally, encroached on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> lands, Madhavrao took command again and defeated the Nizam's army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rakshasbhuvan">Battle of Rakshasbhuvan</a> on 10 August 1763. He later met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Nizam Ali Khan</a> at Kurumkhed in 1766 and reconciled with him. In 1768 he defeated his uncle, captured him and placed him under house arrest at the Shaniwar Wada.</p><p>From January 1764 his armies campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, taking Dharwad Fort and winning at Rattihalli. A second expedition in 1767 won at Sira and Madhugiri, where he freed Queen Virammaji of the Keladi Nayaka Kingdom, and the Sira Subah was annexed. He declined an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> proposal for British forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Vasai</a> and Sashti. His northern armies took tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a> of Bharatpur in 1770, and in 1771 brought the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> protection. At Shukratal in 1772 they defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a>, of whom over 70,000 were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He fell ill with tuberculosis and spent his last days at the Ganesha Chintamani Temple in Theur, where he died on 18 November 1772; Ramabai committed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> on his funeral pyre. The historian James Grant Duff argued that his death did more to bring about the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> than the defeat at Panipat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madhavrao I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Although recovery followed under Peshwa <b>Madhav Rao I</b> and Mahadji Scindia, succession disputes in Pune and rivalry among the chiefs left the confederacy divided.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.52,73.86&amp;year=1762" aria-label="Pune on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6551 1477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Madhavrao I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7001 1747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7001 1747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=18.52,73.86&amp;year=1762">What was here in 1762? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
